3</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gerhardus</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Kielmann</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Sanou</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The use of research for decision-making in the health sector: the case of "shared care" in Burkina Faso</article-title><source>Lessons in Research to Action and Policy &#x02013; Case studies from seven countries</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>Geneva: The Council of Health Research and Development (COHRED) Working Group on Research to Action and Policy</publisher-name><fpage>19</fpage><lpage>27</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Denzin</surname><given-names>NK</given-names></name></person-group><source>The research act; a theoretical introduction to sociological methods</source><year>1970</year><publisher-name>Chicago: Aldine Pub Co</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Knodel</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Morgan DL</surname></name></person-group><article-title>The design and analysis of focus group studies. A practical approach</article-title><source>Successful focus groups: advancing the state of the art</source><year>1993</year><publisher-name>Newbury Park, Calif.: Sage</publisher-name><fpage>35</fpage><lpage>50</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Silverman</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><source>Interpreting qualitative data: methods for analysing talk, text and interaction</source><year>2001</year><edition>2</edition><publisher-name>London: Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chunharas</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Research to action and policy: the need for a new concept</article-title><source>Lessons in Research to Action and Policy &#x02013; Case studies from seven countries</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>Geneva: The Council on Health Research for Development (COHRED) Working Group on Research to Action and Policy</publisher-name><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>8</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sauerborn</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Nitayarumphong</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Gerhardus</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Strategies to enhance the use of health systems research for health sector reform</article-title><source>Trop Med Int Health</source><year>1999</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>827</fpage><lpage>835</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10632991</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1365-3156.1999.00497.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nutley</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Walter</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Davies</surname><given-names>HTO</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>From Knowing to Doing: A Framework for Understanding the Evidence-into-Practice Agenda</article-title><source>Evaluation</source><year>2003</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>148</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1177/1356389003009002002</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mulrow</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Chalmers I, Altman DG</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Rationale for systematic reviews</article-title><source>Systematic Reviews</source><year>1995</year><publisher-name>London: BMJ Publishing Group</publisher-name><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>8</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Volmink</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Siegfried</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Robertson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Gulmezoglu</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Research synthesis and dissemination as a bridge to knowledge management: the Cochrane Collaboration</article-title><source>Bull World Health Organ</source><year>2004</year><volume>82</volume><fpage>778</fpage><lpage>783</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15643800</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lavis</surname><given-names>JN</given-names></name><name><surname>Robertson</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Woodside</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>McLeod</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name><name><surname>Abelson</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><collab>Knowledge Transfer Study G</collab></person-group><article-title>How can research organizations more effectively transfer research knowledge to decision makers?</article-title><source>Milbank Q</source><year>2003</year><volume>81</volume><fpage>221</fpage><lpage>248</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12841049</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/1468-0009.t01-1-00052</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><citation citation-type="book"><source>Bridging the "Know-Do" Gap, Meeting on Knowledge Translation in Global Health</source><year>2006</year><publisher-name>Geneva: World Health Organization</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Health InterNetwork Access to Research Initiative</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.who.int/hinari/en/"/></citation></ref><ref id="B31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Lavis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Rodriguez</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Woodside</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Denis</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Partnership experiences: involving decision-makers in the research process</article-title><source>J Health Serv Res Policy</source><year>2003</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>26</fpage><lpage>34</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14596745</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1258/135581903322405144</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Polanyi</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Republic of Science &#x02013; its political and economic theory</article-title><source>Minerva</source><year>1962</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>54</fpage><lpage>73</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/BF01101453</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>